1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,265
A cup of tea with no lid.

2
00:00:02,265 --> 00:00:04,505
Hot food in a closed steel tiffin.

3
00:00:04,505 --> 00:00:07,048
Tea in a flask that stays hot all day.

4
00:00:07,448 --> 00:00:08,478
Three containers.

5
00:00:08,478 --> 00:00:10,690
Each one lets a different thing escape.

6
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:14,232
Aarav opens his tiffin at
lunch, in the college corridor.

7
00:00:14,582 --> 00:00:16,879
He shut it tight at seven in the morning.

8
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:17,874
Nothing got out.

9
00:00:17,874 --> 00:00:19,168
But the food is cold.

10
00:00:19,668 --> 00:00:22,412
By the end, you will
know where the heat went.

11
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:24,438
We begin Chapter 5, Thermodynamics.

12
00:00:25,088 --> 00:00:29,954
Thermodynamics is the study of energy,
as heat and work, moving from place

13
00:00:29,954 --> 00:00:30,854
to place.

14
00:00:31,148 --> 00:00:33,549
First, pick the part you want to study.

15
00:00:33,549 --> 00:00:36,840
Like drawing a chalk circle
around your toys on the floor.

16
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:39,839
The part inside the circle is the system.

17
00:00:39,839 --> 00:00:41,858
Here, the food in the tiffin.

18
00:00:42,258 --> 00:00:44,582
Everything outside it is the surroundings.

19
00:00:44,582 --> 00:00:46,821
The air, the corridor, Aarav's hands.

20
00:00:47,221 --> 00:00:49,722
The line between them is the boundary.

21
00:00:49,722 --> 00:00:53,839
It can be a real wall, like the
steel, or an imaginary line.

22
00:00:54,239 --> 00:00:57,469
System plus surroundings
together make the universe.

23
00:00:58,119 --> 00:01:01,812
Now put a hot gas in a
box, on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:02,162 --> 00:01:04,274
Two things can cross the boundary.

25
00:01:04,274 --> 00:01:06,613
Matter, which means the stuff itself.

26
00:01:06,613 --> 00:01:08,158
And energy, like heat.

27
00:01:08,558 --> 00:01:10,798
An open system lets both cross.

28
00:01:10,798 --> 00:01:14,157
Steam leaves the open
cup, and heat leaves too.

29
00:01:14,558 --> 00:01:17,922
A closed system stops matter,
but lets energy through.

30
00:01:17,922 --> 00:01:19,413
That is Aarav's tiffin.

31
00:01:19,813 --> 00:01:23,849
The steel keeps the food in,
but heat flows out into the air.

32
00:01:23,849 --> 00:01:25,192
So the food cools.

33
00:01:25,592 --> 00:01:27,697
An isolated system stops both.

34
00:01:27,697 --> 00:01:29,545
A good flask comes close.

35
00:01:29,545 --> 00:01:32,030
No steam out, almost no heat out.

36
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:35,516
A wall that stops heat
is called adiabatic.

37
00:01:35,516 --> 00:01:38,152
The flask has nearly adiabatic walls.

38
00:01:38,802 --> 00:01:40,879
Next, how do we describe a system?

39
00:01:41,229 --> 00:01:43,393
We use a few things we can measure.

40
00:01:43,393 --> 00:01:46,691
Pressure, volume, temperature,
and the amount in moles.

41
00:01:47,091 --> 00:01:49,663
Together they fix the state of the system.

42
00:01:49,663 --> 00:01:51,826
Like a photo of it at one moment.

43
00:01:52,226 --> 00:01:56,260
A state function depends only on the
state right now, not on how the system

44
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:57,160
got there.

45
00:01:57,391 --> 00:01:59,048
Think of climbing a hill.

46
00:01:59,048 --> 00:02:03,432
Your height at the top is the same,
by the steep path or the long winding

47
00:02:03,432 --> 00:02:04,332
path.

48
00:02:04,347 --> 00:02:06,112
Height is like a state function.

49
00:02:06,112 --> 00:02:08,660
But the distance you
walked depends on the path.

50
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:12,416
Pressure, volume and
temperature are state functions.

51
00:02:12,416 --> 00:02:14,693
Heat and work depend on the path.

52
00:02:14,693 --> 00:02:16,352
They are path functions.

53
00:02:17,002 --> 00:02:17,949
One more pair.

54
00:02:17,949 --> 00:02:20,381
Pour a cup of hot tea into two half cups.

55
00:02:20,731 --> 00:02:21,899
The volume halves.

56
00:02:21,899 --> 00:02:22,979
The mass halves.

57
00:02:22,979 --> 00:02:25,332
They depend on how much stuff there is.

58
00:02:25,332 --> 00:02:26,544
They are extensive.

59
00:02:26,944 --> 00:02:29,351
But each half is still just as hot.

60
00:02:29,351 --> 00:02:31,787
Temperature does not depend on amount.

61
00:02:31,787 --> 00:02:32,981
It is intensive.

62
00:02:33,381 --> 00:02:34,706
Pause and think.

63
00:02:34,706 --> 00:02:37,194
Density is mass divided by volume.

64
00:02:37,194 --> 00:02:39,618
Is density extensive or intensive?

65
00:02:42,818 --> 00:02:43,718
Intensive!

66
00:02:43,598 --> 00:02:47,507
Mass halves and volume halves,
so their ratio stays the same.

67
00:02:48,157 --> 00:02:49,189
Remember this.

68
00:02:49,189 --> 00:02:53,567
A state function cares only about
the start and the end, never the road

69
00:02:53,567 --> 00:02:54,467
between.

70
00:02:54,952 --> 00:02:56,642
Now, one worked example.

71
00:02:56,942 --> 00:03:00,372
A gas starts at three hundred
kelvin and two litres.

72
00:03:00,372 --> 00:03:03,445
It ends at three fifty
kelvin and five litres.

73
00:03:03,845 --> 00:03:04,745
Path one.

74
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:08,761
Warm it straight to three fifty
kelvin, and let it grow to five litres.

75
00:03:09,511 --> 00:03:10,411
Path two.

76
00:03:10,259 --> 00:03:14,591
Cool it to two fifty kelvin, let it
swell to eight litres, then bring it

77
00:03:14,591 --> 00:03:15,767
to the same end.

78
00:03:16,517 --> 00:03:20,454
Change in temperature, delta
T, is final minus initial.

79
00:03:20,454 --> 00:03:22,718
Three fifty minus three hundred.

80
00:03:22,718 --> 00:03:25,202
Plus fifty kelvin, on both paths.

81
00:03:25,952 --> 00:03:27,198
Change in volume.

82
00:03:27,198 --> 00:03:28,346
Five minus two.

83
00:03:28,346 --> 00:03:30,641
Plus three litres, on both paths.

84
00:03:31,391 --> 00:03:33,622
Same start, same end, same change.

85
00:03:33,622 --> 00:03:36,511
But the heat used on the
two paths is different.

86
00:03:37,161 --> 00:03:39,867
The ways a system changes have names too.

87
00:03:40,217 --> 00:03:42,610
Isothermal means constant temperature.

88
00:03:42,610 --> 00:03:45,334
Iso means same, and thermal means heat.

89
00:03:45,734 --> 00:03:48,058
Isobaric means constant pressure.

90
00:03:48,058 --> 00:03:50,327
Isochoric means constant volume.

91
00:03:50,727 --> 00:03:53,529
Adiabatic means no heat goes in or out.

92
00:03:53,929 --> 00:03:57,705
And cyclic means the system
comes back to where it started.

93
00:03:57,705 --> 00:04:00,317
So every state function changes by zero.

94
00:04:00,967 --> 00:04:02,429
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:02,779 --> 00:04:06,085
Many students say a closed
system cannot exchange anything.

96
00:04:06,485 --> 00:04:07,385
No.

97
00:04:06,957 --> 00:04:08,744
Closed stops only matter.

98
00:04:08,744 --> 00:04:10,264
Energy still crosses.

99
00:04:10,264 --> 00:04:12,688
Only an isolated system stops both.

100
00:04:13,338 --> 00:04:14,760
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:18,961
Expect quick questions that classify
systems, pick out state functions,

102
00:04:18,961 --> 00:04:21,596
or sort intensive and
extensive properties.

103
00:04:22,196 --> 00:04:23,400
Exam level now.

104
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:26,064
Which set contains only state functions?

105
00:04:26,064 --> 00:04:27,319
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:32,719 --> 00:04:34,014
The answer is B.

107
00:04:34,014 --> 00:04:36,264
Pressure, volume and temperature.

108
00:04:36,764 --> 00:04:38,524
Heat, q, depends on the path.

109
00:04:38,524 --> 00:04:39,739
So A and C are out.

110
00:04:40,239 --> 00:04:42,627
Work, w, also depends on the path.

111
00:04:42,627 --> 00:04:43,640
So D is out.

112
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:46,453
Only B has state functions alone.

113
00:04:46,453 --> 00:04:50,833
Heat and work are energy on the
move, never stored in a system.

114
00:04:51,433 --> 00:04:52,973
Here is your recap.

115
00:04:52,973 --> 00:04:54,556
Screenshot this card.

116
00:04:57,756 --> 00:04:59,942
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:05:00,342 --> 00:05:01,242
One.

118
00:05:00,862 --> 00:05:05,276
Why is a closed tiffin a closed
system, and not an isolated one?

119
00:05:06,076 --> 00:05:06,976
Two.

120
00:05:06,497 --> 00:05:10,116
Is your height on a hill like a
state function or a path function?

121
00:05:10,916 --> 00:05:11,816
Three.

122
00:05:11,469 --> 00:05:13,689
Is temperature extensive or intensive?

123
00:05:14,589 --> 00:05:18,726
Next time: why does a bicycle
pump get hot when you pump a tyre?

124
00:05:18,726 --> 00:05:21,685
We find out in the First
Law of Thermodynamics.
